\section{The exact 2-adic valuation}

\begin{theorem}[Exact parity]\label{thm:2adic}
For $p\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, $W(p)\equiv 2\pmod 4$; equivalently $v_2(W(p))=1$, so $a=W(p)/2$ is odd.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $f(x)=\chi(x(1-x^2))$ and $H$ the lower half, $|H|=(p-1)/2$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:half}, $W(p)=2S$ with $S=\sum_{x\in H}f(x)$. Each $f(x)\in\{0,\pm1\}$, so $f(x)\equiv f(x)^2 \pmod 2$ and $S\equiv\sum_{x\in H}f(x)^2\pmod 2$. Now $f(x)^2=1$ unless $x\in\{0,1,-1\}$; of these, $0\notin H$, $1\in H$, and $-1\notin H$ (its least residue is $p-1$). Hence $\sum_{x\in H}f(x)^2=|H|-1=(p-1)/2-1$, which is odd because $4\mid p-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $p\equiv1\pmod4$ there are integers $a$ (odd) and $b$ with $p=a^2+b^2$ and $2a=W(p)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[An observed mod-8 refinement]
In all computed cases $W(p)\equiv 2\pmod 8$ when $p\equiv5\pmod8$ and $W(p)\equiv6\pmod8$ when $p\equiv1\pmod8$. This determines $W(p)$ \emph{modulo} $8$ from $p$ modulo $8$ while leaving $W(p)$ itself free: it is precisely the residual dial part, publicly computable and carrying no factorisation information. We record it as an empirical observation, not as a theorem proved here.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Combining Theorem~\ref{thm:2adic} with multiplicativity: for $N=pq$ with both primes $\equiv1\pmod4$, $v_2(W(N))=2$; more generally the $2$-adic valuation counts the prime factors $\equiv1\pmod4$. For semiprimes this is determined by $N\bmod4$ and so leaks nothing new.
\end{remark}

\section{Exact evaluations: the dial is broken, the floor is reached}

Direct evaluation of the defining sum gives
\[
W(5)=2,\; W(13)=-6,\; W(17)=-2,\; W(29)=10,\; W(41)=-10,\; W(53)=-14,\; W(173)=26,
\]
\[
W(21)=0,\qquad W(85)=-4 .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Not a residue dial: prime level]
There is no function $f$ with $W(p)=f(p\bmod 8)$ for all primes $p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$17\equiv41\equiv1\pmod8$ but $W(17)=-2\ne-10=W(41)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Not a dial mod 4]
There is no $f$ with $W(p)=f(p\bmod4)$ for all primes: $13\equiv17\equiv1\pmod4$ while $W(13)=-6\ne-2=W(17)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Not a residue dial: composite level]
There is no $f$ with $W(N)=f(N\bmod 8)$ for all odd $N$: $21\equiv85\equiv5\pmod8$ while $W(21)=0\ne-4=W(85)$.
\end{theorem}

These are consistent with multiplicativity: $W(85)=W(5)W(17)=-4$ and $W(21)=W(3)W(7)=0$. Within $p\equiv1\pmod8$ the values run $-2,-10,6,-18,14,22,\dots$; within $N\equiv5\pmod8$ the semiprime values include $0$ ($N=21$), $-4$ ($N=85$), $12$ ($N=221$), $-20$ ($N=205$), $-52$ ($N=2941$).

\begin{theorem}[Near-attainment of the Weil bound]
$W(173)=26$ and $26^2=676>0.97\cdot(4\cdot173)$. Hence no inequality $W(p)^2\le c\,p$ with $c<3.9$ holds for all primes.
\end{theorem}

Further attainments abound: $W(293)=34$ with $34^2=1156$ against $4\cdot293=1172$ (ratio $0.986$). By Theorem~\ref{thm:twosq} the bound is nearly attained exactly when the even leg $b$ of $p=a^2+b^2$ is small.

\section{The verdict: three barriers}

Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime with both factors $\equiv1\pmod4$; otherwise $W(N)=0$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:blind}.

\paragraph{Barrier 1: cost.} Evaluating $W(N)$ from the definition requires $\Theta(N)$ Jacobi-symbol evaluations, exponential in $\log N$ and already worse than trial division. There is no known shortcut: computing $W(N)$ quickly would require $W(p)$ and $W(q)$ separately, which by Theorem~\ref{thm:twosq} amounts to the two-square decomposition of each factor---presupposing the factorisation. The fast way to compute the statistic is to already know the answer.

\paragraph{Barrier 2: symmetry.} $W(N)=W(p)W(q)$ is symmetric in the factors, so it cannot distinguish them, and it presents their contributions only in entangled product form. To use it one must first split a product of two unknown integers---a scaled copy of the original problem.

\paragraph{Barrier 3: the Weil floor.} By Corollary~\ref{cor:floor}, $|W(N)|\le4\sqrt N$: the value lives in a set of size $O(\sqrt N)$, while the factorisation ranges over $\Theta(N/\log^2 N)$ possibilities. By pigeonhole the map from factorisations to values is at least $\Omega(\sqrt N/\log^2 N)$-to-one. Theorem~\ref{thm:jac} explains why no rescaling helps: $A(1)^2+A(\nu)^2=4p$ is a conservation law, and a witness reading $A(1)$ reads a projection of a conserved quantity.

\paragraph{Empirical decorrelation.} For a sample of forty semiprimes $N=pq$ with distinct factors $\equiv1\pmod4$ below $400$, the Pearson correlation of $W(N)$ against each of $p,q,p+q,|p-q|$ was compared to a permutation null (2000 reshufflings of the $W$-values). Every observed $|r|$ was below $0.25$, while the 95th percentile of the null lay in $0.28$--$0.32$. The statistic is factor-dependent but unstructured.

\paragraph{A new taxonomy entry.} \emph{Dial collapse} (previous witnesses): $F(N)=f(N\bmod m)$, zero factorisation information. \emph{Weil-floor collapse} (this witness): $F$ escapes every residue dial and is genuinely factor-dependent, yet its total variation is $O(\sqrt N)$, entering only through a symmetric product and a conserved quadratic form. The second obstruction is strictly deeper and survives arbitrary re-weighting by multiplicative characters.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{The general principle}

The proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse} used almost nothing about the weight: summing $y$ away converts the circle count into $\sum_x\psi(x)(1+\chi(1-x^2))$ for a weight $\psi$. For any nontrivial multiplicative $\psi$ the first piece vanishes and the second is, after $t=x^2$, a Jacobi sum---of absolute value exactly $\sqrt p$. Hence: \emph{the $\sqrt p$ floor is a property of the circle, not of the weight}. One does not escape it by changing the character; one would have to change the geometry.

\subsection{What was gained}

A clean, self-contained elementary proof of the Weil bound for $y^2=x^3-x$ via an exact second moment over quadratic twists; an exact identity $A(1)^2+A(\nu)^2=4p$ yielding Fermat's two-square theorem with explicit computable witnesses; and a refutation with a mechanism that generalises.

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Every character-weighted circle count is at the floor]
Let $\psi$ be a nontrivial multiplicative character mod $p$ of order $d$ and $W_\psi(p)=\sum_{x^2+y^2=1}\psi(x)$. Then $|W_\psi(p)|\le d\sqrt p$, and $W_\psi(p)$ is a $\Z[\zeta_d]$-linear combination of traces of Frobenius of the curves $y^2=x^3-cx$ (for $d=2$) or of Jacobi sums $J(\psi,\chi)$ in general; no choice of $\psi$ escapes the square-root floor.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[The two-leg trade-off is a hard obstruction]
For semiprimes $N=pq$ the pair of residue and nonresidue legs is equidistributed on the ``Weil circle'' $u^2+v^2\le 16N$, so that $N\mapsto W(N)/(4\sqrt N)$ has limiting distribution the Sato--Tate-type pushforward of a product of two arcsine laws---hence carries $O(1)$ bits about $p$ versus $q$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[2-adic valuation as a complete obstruction]
For $N=pq$ with both primes $\equiv1\pmod4$, $v_2(W(N))=2$ exactly, and in general $v_2(W(N))$ equals the number of prime factors of $N$ congruent to $1$ mod $4$---a genuine but already publicly computable invariant.
\end{conjecture}

Beyond these: since the floor is a property of the geometry, the natural next probe is a family of curves whose point counts are not governed by a single Frobenius eigenvalue pair---higher genus, or non-CM families---subject always to the binding cost constraint that a useful witness must be computable in time $o(\sqrt N)$. Finally, proving the observed mod-$8$ sign normalisation would isolate exactly the residual ``dial part'' of the statistic and quantify how much of it is public.

\section{Conclusion}

The Jacobi-signed circle count is the first witness in its family to escape residue-dial collapse: it is provably not a function of $N\bmod 8$, nor of $N\bmod4$, at either prime or composite level, and it is multiplicative, hence genuinely factor-dependent. It is nonetheless useless for factoring, for reasons we can state exactly: cost $\Theta(N)$, a symmetric product entangling the factors, and the Weil floor $|W(N)|\le4\sqrt N$---a bound that is not an estimate but the shadow of the exact conservation law $A(1)^2+A(\nu)^2=4p$. That law identifies the statistic as twice the odd leg of the two-square decomposition of $p$, giving a fully explicit character-sum proof that every prime $p\equiv1\pmod4$ is a sum of two squares.

\end{document}

